d its CheckRun for timing.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-accept-reply.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 45 opened a new lead on the accept handler's dead rejection path and submitted its first check.\\n\\nThe lead. handleAcceptReply has a branch for an AcceptReply whose ballot exceeds the round's ballot — increment nacks and, once a majority has nacked, make a new ballot and re-run prepare — but the handler returns earlier when lb.lastTriedBallot != areply.Ballot, so that branch cannot run. A peer with a higher stored bal produces exactly that reply, because handleAccept prints a smaller-ballot message, leaves the record alone and answers with its own bal. The check's harness builds the leader's ACCEPTED round with the package's own instance and bookkeeping constructors and delivers such a reply: the local construction run reports nacks 0, accept_oks 0, no message emitted and the round unchanged, so the leader never learns it was rejected.\\n\\nObligation and check (new claim C7-accept-reply-higher-ballot-is-a-rejection, bounded liveness, priority 2). The duty is that a reply whose ballot exceeds the accept round's ballot must be recorded as a rejection, so the leader can make a new ballot and prepare again; the check asserts nacks == 1 on the reply event with the accept round as a prerequisite alias and instance identity correlation, and grounds the claim in the handler's own branch plus the accept handler's reply value, with a new binding (BB8-accept-reply). The claim's scope is one reply and one round; the timeout-driven recovery path, which can still advance the instance, and the safety irrelevance of ignoring the reply are stated in counterevidence, so the consequence is bounded to a delay on the collision path rather than a stall or an agreement failure.\\n\\nRelated source observation, recorded as an unknown rather than a claim: on the pre-accept side the rejection signal cannot exist either, because PreAcceptReply copies the request's ballot and handlePreAccept returns silently when the request's ballot is too small — so the only rejection signal in the protocol is the absence of a reply, which the leader cannot distinguish from message loss, and progress relies on the 10s execution timeout. Audit spec version 9; the six settled claims are untouched, the two paused candidates keep their resume conditions, and no requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened.\"}"}}
